const e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"CR_E3_001","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"All the success stories of achievers around the globe at all times, show that all of them had undergone several failures and they finally succeeded only because of their persistent efforts and never-say-die spirit. Thus, if anyone ignores failures and perseveres in her chosen mission she is bound to succeed and achieve success in life.","question":"Which of the following, if true, casts the most serious doubt on the above conclusion?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"While stories of successful persons come out in the open, those of failed ones who also toil never see the light of the day.","B":"There are still a number of achievers worldwide who remain far removed from public glare.","C":"After achieving success, people reap more and more successes in their lives.","D":"There are many achievers whose feats are not acclaimed during their lifetime."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"While stories of successful persons come out in the open, those of failed ones who also toil never see the light of the day.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"B":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The argument concludes that perseverance guarantees success, based on the success stories of achievers. We need to weaken this. Choice A: CORRECT — this points out a survivorship bias: we only hear about those who persevered and succeeded, not about those who also persevered but still failed. This suggests that people can fail despite persistent efforts, directly casting doubt on the conclusion. Choice B: Remaining obscure does not affect whether a person has succeeded or not — this is irrelevant to the conclusion. Choice C: Success leading to more success has no relevance to whether perseverance guarantees success. Choice D: Lack of acclamation during one's lifetime does not mean the person did not succeed — this does not weaken the conclusion.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["weaken"]},{"id":"CR_E3_002","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"According to a new study, when a person sneezes he spreads sufficient virus to cause the common cold in 15000 persons around in just 10 minutes time. Hence doctors advise people to cover their nose with a handkerchief when a person nearby is sneezing.","question":"Which of the following, if true, casts the most serious doubt on the doctor's advice to safeguard oneself from catching a cold from a person sneezing?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"No protection is needed when we are sufficiently far away from the person sneezing.","B":"A handkerchief has small holes between its threads larger than the size of the virus.","C":"It may be considered bad manners to cover the nose with a handkerchief when one is in the midst of a group of people.","D":"Time may not be sufficient to cover the nose with a handkerchief after finding a person sneezing nearby."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"A handkerchief has small holes between its threads larger than the size of the virus.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope","D":"too_extreme"},"explanation":"The doctor's advice is to cover the nose with a handkerchief for protection against the virus. We need to show this protective measure is ineffective. Choice A: The scenario of being far from the sneezing person is outside the scope of the argument, which concerns being nearby. Choice B: CORRECT — if the holes in the handkerchief's threads are larger than the virus, the handkerchief cannot block the virus and therefore provides no protection, directly undermining the doctor's advice. Choice C: Social manners are beyond the purview of the argument about medical protection. Choice D: The study states the virus spreads in about 10 minutes, so there is time to cover one's nose — this does not seriously undermine the advice.","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"question_type":["weaken"]},{"id":"CR_E3_003","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"Environmentalists decry the use of plastic chairs and other furniture made of plastics. This is because plastics are bio-non-degradable and hence contribute to environmental degradation. So, it is better to use wooden furniture in the place of plastic furniture to prevent environmental degradation.","question":"Which of the following, if true, casts the most serious doubt on the above conclusion regarding the use of wooden furniture in the place of plastic furniture to prevent environmental degradation?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Wood is biodegradable.","B":"Smoke by burning wood also causes air pollution.","C":"Plastic furniture is much cheaper and lighter compared to wooden furniture.","D":"Felling of trees for wood causes deforestation."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Felling of trees for wood causes deforestation.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"confuses_weakener_with_assumption","B":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The argument concludes that switching to wooden furniture prevents environmental degradation. We need information showing wooden furniture also harms the environment. Choice A: Wood being biodegradable actually strengthens the conclusion that wood is better for the environment. Choice B: The argument is not about burning wood — this is irrelevant to the conclusion about furniture use. Choice C: Price and weight of plastic furniture are irrelevant to environmental degradation. Choice D: CORRECT — if producing wooden furniture requires felling trees causing deforestation, then using wooden furniture also causes environmental degradation, directly undermining the conclusion that it is better for the environment.","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"question_type":["weaken"]},{"id":"CR_E3_004","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"John has five children – a, b, c, d and e – not necessarily in that order. c, the eldest is a son. b and e are twins – one a male and the other a female again not necessarily in the same order.","question":"Which of the following is the minimum additional information required which can help in answering the question, 'How many daughters does John have?'","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"John has more sons than daughters.","B":"e, the youngest is female.","C":"d is the last son of John.","D":"John has more daughters than sons."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"John has more daughters than sons.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"correct_but_unstated","B":"correct_but_unstated","C":"correct_but_unstated"},"explanation":"From the given information: c is a son; b and e are twins with one male and one female; nothing is known about a and d. So among c, b, and e there are two sons and one daughter. a and d are unknown. Choice A: John having more sons than daughters allows for either 3 or 4 sons and 1 or 2 daughters — this does not uniquely determine the number of daughters. Choice B: Knowing e is female tells us b is male, but nothing is known about a — this is still insufficient. Choice C: d being the last son does not give information about a and e's genders — insufficient. Choice D: CORRECT — if John has more daughters than sons, and we know at minimum two of c/b/e are sons, John must have exactly 3 daughters (a, d, and whichever of b/e is female), giving a unique answer.","time_benchmark_seconds":105,"question_type":["conclusion"]},{"id":"CR_E3_005","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"40% of the population in a country is female. 60% of the males in the country are literate and 70% of the females are illiterate.","question":"If the particulars given above are true, which of the following must also be true?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"The female illiterates in the country are fewer in number than the male illiterates.","B":"The total literates in the country are more than the total illiterates.","C":"The total males in the country are fewer in number than the total females in the country.","D":"The literate males are more in number than the illiterate females."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"The literate males are more in number than the illiterate females.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","B":"contradicts_passage","C":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"Taking population as 100: 60 males and 40 females. Among males: 36 literate, 24 illiterate. Among females: 12 literate, 28 illiterate. Choice A: Female illiterates = 28, male illiterates = 24. Female illiterates are actually more, not fewer — this is false. Choice B: Total literates = 48, total illiterates = 52. Illiterates outnumber literates — this is false. Choice C: Males = 60, females = 40. Males are more numerous, not fewer — this is false. Choice D: CORRECT — literate males = 36, illiterate females = 28. Literate males are indeed more in number than illiterate females.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["inference"]},{"id":"CR_E3_006","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"Medical research on cure for certain deadly diseases is funded liberally by the government from out of general tax revenue. Certain cities insist that the cost of such medical research must be collected only from those who undergo treatment evolving from such research.","question":"Which of the following forms the basis for the objection of the cities to the government funding of medical research?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"There are other more important needs which must be met with government's resources.","B":"Medical research involves huge costs and hence government cannot be expected to fund it exclusively.","C":"Medical research generally involves experimenting with guinea pigs and hence government funding it is unethical.","D":"Only the end user of any facility must bear all the cost of a facility."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Only the end user of any facility must bear all the cost of a facility.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The cities object to all taxpayers funding research that benefits only those who suffer from certain diseases. We need the underlying assumption of their objection. Choice A: Other important needs are outside the purview of the argument. Choice B: The argument does not refer to the size of costs — this is irrelevant. Choice C: No information is given about the nature of the research — this is irrelevant. Choice D: CORRECT — the cities' objection is based on the principle that since only those who suffer from deadly diseases benefit from the research, only they should bear the expenses. This is the foundational assumption underlying their position.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["assumption"]},{"id":"CR_E3_007","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"Country X is the largest producer of coffee accounting for nearly one fourth of the global production. However, coffee is not among the major items of export from the country.","question":"Which of the following, if true, cannot be the reason for the fact that country X is not a major exporter of coffee?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"The quality of coffee grown in the country is inferior compared to most other coffees sold in global markets.","B":"The price of coffee produced in X is at least 25% more than that of coffee exported by major exporters of coffee.","C":"Coffee produced in country X has a tendency to become tasteless in a few days after it is picked from plants unless preserved at high cost.","D":"The people of the country X prefer tea to coffee."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"The people of the country X prefer tea to coffee.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"confuses_weakener_with_assumption","B":"confuses_weakener_with_assumption","C":"confuses_weakener_with_assumption"},"explanation":"We need to find what cannot be a reason for coffee not being a major export. Any negative characteristic of the coffee as an export product would be a valid reason. Choice A: Inferior quality is a valid reason why X's coffee would not sell well in global markets — this can be a reason. Choice B: Being 25% more expensive than competitors is a valid reason for low exports — this can be a reason. Choice C: Coffee becoming tasteless quickly unless expensively preserved is a valid reason for difficulty in exporting — this can be a reason. Choice D: CORRECT — the domestic preference for tea over coffee relates to domestic consumption, not to export competitiveness. Even if people of X prefer tea, the country could still export its large coffee production. This cannot be a reason for low exports.","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"question_type":["conclusion"]},{"id":"CR_E3_008","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"Several farmers in the U.S. have, of late, switched over to organic farming from using synthetic fertilizers and pesticides, whose costs are rising rapidly. Organic farming also helps in replenishing the nutrients in the soil. However, organic farming results in reduced yields in the first few years of switching over. Despite this, no farmer who has switched over to organic farming has reported any fall in income in the initial years of switching over or subsequently.","question":"Which of the following, if true, can offer an explanation for the above?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"The yields, when organic farming is resorted to, increase considerably after five or six years.","B":"As the farmers who have switched over to organic farming have done so more because of environmental concerns than because of income considerations, they do not mind the loss in income.","C":"Farmers who have been doing chemical farming use costly methods which they cannot discard easily and switch over to organic farming.","D":"Consumers are prepared to pay more for produce from organic farming because they know that those from chemical farming are harmful to their health."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Consumers are prepared to pay more for produce from organic farming because they know that those from chemical farming are harmful to their health.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"We need to explain why farmers maintain income despite reduced yields after switching to organic farming. Choice A: Yields increasing after five to six years concerns the future, not the initial years — this does not explain the current situation. Choice B: Farmers not minding a loss in income contradicts the stated fact that there is no fall in income — this does not explain it. Choice C: Other farmers being unable to switch is irrelevant to why those who did switch maintained their income. Choice D: CORRECT — if consumers pay a premium for organic produce, farmers can earn the same or more income despite lower yields, directly explaining why no fall in income is reported.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["paradox_resolution"]},{"id":"CR_E3_009","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"Researchers repeatedly aver that high blood pressure (hypertension) results if there is a large presence of salt. Normally foods consumed by people in the tropical countries contain a much higher content of salts than those consumed by those in the cold countries. Thus, it can be expected that high blood pressure is more prevalent among people in the tropics than those in temperate regions. However, statistics do not show any marked variation in the prevalence of high blood pressure in the two regions.","question":"Which of the following, if true, can offer the most possible explanation for the above contradiction?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"People in the tropics are also large consumers of food items like fruits which contain less salt.","B":"The climatic conditions in the tropics lead to profuse sweating resulting in loss of salts and water.","C":"Though people in the cold climates do not consume salty preparations due to health considerations, they are nevertheless fond of such foods.","D":"More number of people in the tropical countries suffer from heart diseases for which one of the causes is hypertension."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"The climatic conditions in the tropics lead to profuse sweating resulting in loss of salts and water.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"correct_but_unstated","C":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"We need to explain why higher salt consumption in tropical countries does not lead to higher rates of hypertension. Choice A: Eating low-salt fruits does not fully discount the possibility of still developing high blood pressure from the high-salt foods also consumed. Choice B: CORRECT — profuse sweating in tropical climates causes loss of salts from the body, offsetting the higher salt intake and explaining why hypertension rates do not differ markedly between tropical and cold regions. Choice C: People in cold climates being fond of salty foods but not consuming them is not going to raise their salt levels and does not explain the paradox. Choice D: Heart disease prevalence is outside the scope of the argument, which is specifically about hypertension rates.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["paradox_resolution"]},{"id":"CR_E3_010","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"A vast majority of movie-buffs desire to see a movie as soon as possible after its release. Watching a movie in the comfort of home is always more welcome than watching it in cinema halls. However, telecasting the movie has not caused any dent in the initial days of release of a movie.","question":"Which of the following, if true, can offer a suitable explanation for the attendance not coming down in cinema halls in the initial days of release of a movie?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Law prohibits the screening of any movie in the television within a year of its release.","B":"Watching a movie on a television costs less than watching it in a cinema hall.","C":"The attendance for a cinema depends on the popularity of the stars cast in the movie.","D":"The owner of the rights to a movie earns more when it is telecast through the television from the commercials shown in between."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Law prohibits the screening of any movie in the television within a year of its release.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"B":"contradicts_passage","C":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"We need to explain why, despite people preferring home viewing, cinema attendance is not affected in the initial days of release. Choice A: CORRECT — if law prohibits telecasting any movie within a year of its release, new movies simply cannot be watched at home, which fully explains why cinema attendance is unaffected in the initial days. Choice B: Lower cost of home viewing would make home viewing more attractive, which would reduce cinema attendance — this contradicts the situation described. Choice C: Star popularity affects attendance levels but does not explain why telecasting fails to reduce it. Choice D: Rights owners' earnings are irrelevant to movie-goers' preference for cinema halls.","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"question_type":["paradox_resolution"]},{"id":"CR_E3_011","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"Almost all the textile shops in St. Benedict town have reported a sharp increase in sales of a particular design of coats worn by women - one that's totally different from the design a huge majority of women in the town have been wearing until recently. This shows that coats of the new fashion are a craze with the women in the town and we can expect a massive switchover to the new type shortly.","question":"The argument above is properly drawn if the truth of which of the following is assured?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Fashions change every now and then and women generally adopt the latest fashion.","B":"St. Benedict is a town where cultural changes occur very rapidly.","C":"The coats sold in the various shops of the town are meant to be used locally.","D":"The prices of coats of the new design are affordable for the women of the town."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"The coats sold in the various shops of the town are meant to be used locally.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"out_of_scope","D":"correct_but_unstated"},"explanation":"The argument concludes that the sales increase in shops indicates a craze among women in the town. We need the assumption that makes this conclusion valid. Choice A: The general tendency for fashions to change is not an assumption that specifically links the sales data to a local craze. Choice B: The rate of cultural change in the town is not the needed assumption for the conclusion to hold. Choice C: CORRECT — if the coats sold in local shops are not meant for local use (e.g., purchased by tourists or for export), the sales increase would not indicate a local craze. The conclusion is valid only if the sales reflect local consumption. Choice D: Affordability would already be indicated by the actual sales increase, so it is not a necessary additional assumption.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["assumption"]},{"id":"CR_E3_012","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"India's population is expected to increase by 100 and 150 million by 2025 and 2040 A.D. respectively. This will require 240 and 325 million tonnes of food grains respectively. Taking the current production of food into consideration, it can be said that India needs to raise its food production by 5 million tonnes per year. Since the land-to-man ratio is narrowing rapidly, there is almost no scope for horizontal expansion to meet the projected demand of the future.","question":"Which of the following can be a solution to this problem?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"India should now start importing food grains from other countries.","B":"The production of other crops should be cut down and food production given utmost importance.","C":"Cross breeding to improve the quality of food grain should be encouraged.","D":"The government should adopt new technologies to increase the production of food grains."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"The government should adopt new technologies to increase the production of food grains.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"too_extreme","C":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The problem is increasing food demand with no room for horizontal expansion of farmland. We need a solution that increases production within existing land. Choice A: Importing food grains depends on foreign exchange and many other external factors — this is not a reliable or self-sufficient solution. Choice B: Cutting other crops to focus on food production is not an apt solution as it would hurt other agricultural sectors. Choice C: Cross-breeding improves quality, not quantity — this does not address the production shortfall. Choice D: CORRECT — adopting new technologies to increase yields offers a way to raise food production without expanding the land area, which is the precise constraint identified in the argument.","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"question_type":["conclusion"]},{"id":"CR_E3_013","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"It is a well-known fact, duly supported by statistics compiled by leading research agencies, that more and more people are building residential properties in the suburbs than in the heart of a metro. So, in order to boost its sagging sales, Bheem Cable Internet should try to focus more on the suburban market than on the centrally located markets.","question":"What does the above argument assume?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"There is no movement of people from suburbs to the central areas.","B":"Bheem Cable's sales have declined recently.","C":"Sale and purchase of property is a crucial parameter to be considered in marketing Internet connections.","D":"Purchasers of property in the suburbs do not keep the property idle."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Purchasers of property in the suburbs do not keep the property idle.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"premise_restatement","C":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The argument assumes that building more properties in the suburbs translates to a larger customer base for internet connections there. Choice A: Movement of people between areas does not affect the core assumption that suburban property buyers become suburban residents needing internet. Choice B: Bheem Cable's sales being sagging is already stated in the argument as a premise, not an assumption. Choice C: The relationship between property purchase and internet marketing is a general claim, not the specific assumption needed here. Choice D: CORRECT — if suburban property buyers keep the property idle (e.g., as investment), the population of the suburb does not grow and there is no expanded market for internet services. The argument only works if the newly constructed properties are actually occupied.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["assumption"]},{"id":"CR_E3_014","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"There is a price to pay for nuclear independence, said the Finance Minister grappling with the problem of fixing the price and how to make the nation pay it; how to make the world, the NRIs and everyone else come to the aid of the party. He knew that there would not be global sanctions, only national hiccups. But with an economy on the slippery slope of recession, even hiccups could cause upheaval. So, even as the people were in an upbeat mood, the economy was on a slide. The Pokhran fallout may have been politically beneficial but it was economically disturbing.","question":"Which of the following is true according to the paragraph?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"The nuclear test in Pokhran has proved harmful to the flow of foreign investment into India.","B":"The India Development Fund has failed to meet the requirements of the Indian economy.","C":"The Pokhran nuclear test proved to be economically helpful, though politically disturbing.","D":"The economy in India was on the verge of a recession, and the Pokhran experiment could have been the last straw."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"The economy in India was on the verge of a recession, and the Pokhran experiment could have been the last straw.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"out_of_scope","C":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"We need to identify what is directly supported by the passage. Choice A: The passage makes no reference to the flow of foreign direct investment into India. Choice B: The India Development Fund is not mentioned anywhere in the passage. Choice C: This reverses what the passage states — the passage says the Pokhran fallout was politically beneficial but economically disturbing, not the other way around. Choice D: CORRECT — the passage states the economy was already on the slippery slope of recession, and that even small hiccups from the Pokhran tests could cause upheaval. The Pokhran experiment was the additional economic burden on an already fragile economy.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["inference"]},{"id":"CR_E3_015","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"During the last ten years, 35 percent of those who passed from management schools were women. All those who passed from management schools have secured jobs. However, of all the persons who passed from management schools and who are employed, women form only seven percent.","question":"Which of the following, if true, can explain the difference in percentages mentioned in the above passage?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Each year, the top few ranks from the management schools were secured by men.","B":"Population of women has gone down from 60 percent to 51 percent in the country in the last ten years.","C":"Majority of those from management schools and who are in jobs now were employed more than 10 years ago, at which time nearly 95 percent of the batch were men.","D":"Women are unable to secure jobs which pay them a wage equal to what a man would be paid."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Majority of those from management schools and who are in jobs now were employed more than 10 years ago, at which time nearly 95 percent of the batch were men.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"out_of_scope","D":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"We need to explain why women are 35% of recent graduates but only 7% of all currently employed management school alumni. Choice A: Academic rank is irrelevant — all those who passed secured jobs regardless of rank. Choice B: The country's overall population demographics are irrelevant to management school employment figures. Choice C: CORRECT — if the majority of currently employed management school alumni were hired more than 10 years ago, when women made up only about 5% of batches, the overall employed pool would be dominated by men. The 35% female ratio only applies to recent graduates who are a minority of the total employed pool. Choice D: The passage explicitly states all those who passed secured jobs — women being unable to secure jobs contradicts this stated fact.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["paradox_resolution"]},{"id":"CR_E3_016","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"People of a nation have suddenly started suffering from a strange viral attack. The virus, on attacking a person, multiplies quickly and creates serious problems, sometimes proving fatal. The symptoms of the disease, however, become apparent only five days after the virus attacks a person. Very effective medicine to kill the virus is available in the market. Nevertheless, doctors are unable to cure the viral disease and prevent death.","question":"Which of the following, if true, accounts for the above situation?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"The cost of medicine is beyond the reach of the poor people of the country.","B":"The multiplication of the virus inside the body is so fast that it requires sustained medication for at least a week to cure the disease.","C":"The medicine available in the market is effective only if administered within two days of the viral attack.","D":"As the viral disease is relatively new, some of the doctors are unable to diagnose it correctly."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"The medicine available in the market is effective only if administered within two days of the viral attack.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"correct_but_unstated","B":"out_of_scope","D":"correct_but_unstated"},"explanation":"We need to explain why effective medicine exists yet doctors cannot cure the disease. Choice A: High cost would prevent some but not all patients from getting treatment — this cannot explain the universal inability to cure. Choice B: Requiring sustained medication for a week does not explain why the medicine fails — doctors could still administer it for a week. Choice C: CORRECT — symptoms only appear after 5 days, but the medicine only works if given within 2 days of viral attack. By the time symptoms appear and patients seek treatment, the 2-day window has already passed, making the medicine ineffective. This fully accounts for the situation. Choice D: Some doctors being unable to diagnose correctly could only partially explain the situation, not account for all cases of inability to cure.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["paradox_resolution"]},{"id":"CR_E3_017","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"More than half of the firms employing U.S labor do not strictly follow the nine to five, five-day week schedule stipulated for labor. This is attributable to the sharp increase in the number of firms in the service sector and also the personnel in the service sector gradually outnumbering those in other sectors.","question":"Which of the following, if true, could explain how the violation of the work hours stipulation has been due to the growth of the service sector?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Most of the jobs in the service sector are related to leisure activities which people normally indulge in outside the nine to five schedule.","B":"The personnel employed in the service sector are paid much more than those in other sectors.","C":"The scope for a drastic increase in the number of jobs exists only in the service sector now.","D":"Persons employed in sectors other than the service sector have more physical work to do and hence cannot work for more hours beyond the nine to five schedule."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Most of the jobs in the service sector are related to leisure activities which people normally indulge in outside the nine to five schedule.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"B":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"We need to explain how the nature of service sector growth causes violation of standard work hour stipulations. Choice A: CORRECT — if service sector jobs are related to leisure activities (restaurants, entertainment, hospitality) which people engage in outside 9 to 5, then by the very nature of these jobs, employees must work outside the stipulated schedule. This directly explains how service sector growth leads to work-hour violations. Choice B: Higher pay in the service sector is not relevant to why work hours deviate from the stipulated schedule. Choice C: The potential for job growth only explains the sector's expansion, not why its workers violate hour stipulations. Choice D: Physical limitations of other sectors' workers are irrelevant to the service sector's schedule violations.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["paradox_resolution"]},{"id":"CR_E3_018","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"Artificial knee implants, thought to be a safe procedure till now, may cause cancer in patients 30 years after the implants, according to a new study. Though such implants effectively help to improve the quality of life of the recipients, they are not advisable because of the cancer they are likely to cause.","question":"Which of the following, if true, can be a counter argument to the above argument?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Other implants are also believed to result in some undesirable consequences but still they are performed.","B":"The patients even after knowing the risk involved insist on knee implants.","C":"In some of the cases where implants have caused cancer the affliction has not proved fatal.","D":"Almost all the patients in whom knee implants are done are old and the probability of their living beyond 20 years is low."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Almost all the patients in whom knee implants are done are old and the probability of their living beyond 20 years is low.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The argument concludes that knee implants are inadvisable because they may cause cancer 30 years later. We need a counter argument. Choice A: Other implants having undesirable consequences is outside the scope — the argument concerns only knee implants. Choice B: Patient willingness to accept risk does not eliminate the risk itself and does not counter the medical argument against implants. Choice C: Cancer not proving fatal in some cases does not address whether the implants should be recommended. Choice D: CORRECT — if patients receiving knee implants are typically old with a low probability of living beyond 20 years, they are extremely unlikely to survive to the 30-year point when cancer might develop. The cancer risk is therefore practically irrelevant, directly countering the argument against implants.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["weaken"]},{"id":"CR_E3_019","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"JOHN: Mr X has been awarded the Nobel prize for chemistry this year. It is right on the part of country 'K' to claim that the prize has been awarded to it, as the research work for which Mr. X was awarded the prize was carried out by him entirely in the last 10 years while staying in K, where he has taken his citizenship. PAUL: But Mr. X was born in country 'T' and he had his entire education there before he moved to 'K' and hence 'T' is more entitled to the claim.","question":"Which of the following relationships does Paul's response have to John's argument?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Paul explains why John's argument is totally unfounded.","B":"Paul counters John's argument by suggesting a basis he thinks is fairer to adjudge the claim.","C":"Paul agrees with John's argument but differs from him only on the basis of his reasoning.","D":"Paul suggests that a person's place of birth and education play a significant part in a person's academic achievement."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Paul counters John's argument by suggesting a basis he thinks is fairer to adjudge the claim.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"too_extreme","C":"contradicts_passage","D":"correct_but_unstated"},"explanation":"John argues K deserves credit based on where the research was done and citizenship. Paul argues T deserves credit based on birth and education. We need to characterise Paul's response. Choice A: Paul does not totally discount John's claim — he accepts the same type of argument (national claim to a Nobel) but proposes a different basis. 'Totally unfounded' is too extreme. Choice B: CORRECT — Paul accepts that national credit is a valid concept but counters John by proposing birth and education as a fairer basis than citizenship and place of research. Choice C: Paul does not agree with John — he proposes a different country as the rightful claimant. Choice D: While Paul's argument implies this, this option does not capture that Paul is countering John's argument about which country deserves the credit.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["inference"]},{"id":"CR_E3_020","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"As a consequence of the phenomenal expansion of the service sector last year the job openings in organizations of the service sector registered a steep rise and were many times more than those in other sectors. However, a survey among students who passed out of colleges last year indicated that a higher percentage of students who pursued courses relevant to the manufacturing sector secured jobs, than those who did courses catering to the service sector.","question":"Which of the following, if true, can be a proper explanation for the above happening?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Few among those who undertook courses leading to jobs in the service sector went for higher education.","B":"While almost all employers in the service sector stipulated experience of at least 1 year as a necessary qualification, employers in other sectors were satisfied with academic qualifications alone.","C":"A sizeable number of students who pursued courses leading to jobs in the service sector landed jobs in other sectors.","D":"A good majority of students who had passed out of colleges in earlier years secured jobs last year."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"While almost all employers in the service sector stipulated experience of at least 1 year as a necessary qualification, employers in other sectors were satisfied with academic qualifications alone.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","C":"contradicts_passage","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"We need to explain why service sector course graduates had lower job placement rates despite more service sector job openings. Choice A: Few service sector course graduates going for higher education does not explain why the rest failed to secure jobs. Choice B: CORRECT — if service sector employers require at least 1 year of experience while other sectors accept fresh graduates on academic merit, fresh service sector graduates would be disqualified from service sector jobs, explaining their lower placement rate despite abundant openings. Choice C: Service sector course students landing jobs in other sectors would actually increase their placement percentage, contradicting the paradox. Choice D: Earlier graduates securing jobs last year does not explain the relative performance of the two groups of fresh graduates.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["paradox_resolution"]},{"id":"CR_E3_021","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"John: Jim is eminently qualified to be the President of the country, as he is a scholar and also has experience as a senator for nearly fifteen years. Jack: But Jim is not likely to be elected President as the poor apprehend that his policies may hurt them.","question":"Jack's argument depends upon which of the following?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Being a scholar and having experience will not help Jim in functioning as the President of the country.","B":"Jim comes from an affluent family.","C":"The poor do not vote for those who are academically well-qualified.","D":"The poor of the country constitute a deciding factor in the outcome of the Presidential election."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"The poor of the country constitute a deciding factor in the outcome of the Presidential election.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Jack claims Jim is unlikely to be elected because the poor distrust his policies. For this to be a valid argument, there must be an underlying assumption about the poor's electoral influence. Choice A: Whether scholarship and experience help in functioning as President is not related to Jack's argument about electability. Choice B: Jim's family background is not related to Jack's argument. Choice C: Academic qualification is not the stated basis for the poor's distrust — their concern is his policies, not his credentials. Choice D: CORRECT — Jack's argument only holds if the poor have sufficient voting power to determine the election outcome. If the poor were not a deciding factor, their apprehension about Jim's policies would be electorally irrelevant.","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"question_type":["assumption"]},{"id":"CR_E3_022","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"Allergy is the reaction of the body to the entry of a foreign entity. A recent study has revealed that food allergy is more common in country X than in country Y. However, more people of country Y were consumers of foods that usually cause allergic reactions (such as milk, fish etc) than those of country X.","question":"Which of the following, if true, could offer an explanation to the contradiction above?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Country X is smitten with antibacterial detergents and hand sanitizers which incapacitate the immune system leading it to misidentify certain foods as foreign.","B":"Allergy is also caused by factors other than the presence of foreign elements in food.","C":"Country Y has got more doctors, who specialize in treating food-allergy-related diseases than country X.","D":"People of country Y have been consumers of allergy-causing foods for a long time and there has been no known evidence of deaths caused by allergic reactions to food."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Country X is smitten with antibacterial detergents and hand sanitizers which incapacitate the immune system leading it to misidentify certain foods as foreign.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"B":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"We need to explain why country X has more food allergies despite fewer people consuming allergy-causing foods. We need a mechanism that increases allergic reactions in X independent of the usual allergens. Choice A: CORRECT — overuse of antibacterial detergents and sanitizers weakens the immune system's ability to distinguish real foreign threats from harmless foods, causing it to misidentify common foods as foreign entities and trigger allergic reactions. This explains higher food allergy rates in X despite lower consumption of traditionally allergenic foods. Choice B: Other causes of allergy being mentioned does not specifically explain why X has more food allergies than Y. Choice C: More specialist doctors in Y relates to treatment capacity, not to the occurrence of food allergies. Choice D: Y's population having long exposure to allergenic foods without deaths is interesting context but does not explain why X has higher food allergy rates.","time_benchmark_seconds":105,"question_type":["paradox_resolution"]},{"id":"CR_E3_023","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"Humidity refers to the presence of water vapour in the atmosphere around us. It is surprising that the higher the humidity at a place, the greater people seem to sweat.","question":"Which of the following, if true, can be a logical explanation for the above phenomenon?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"A higher temperature evaporates the water vapours in the atmosphere.","B":"People sweat only when they exert themselves physically.","C":"Presence of water vapour in the atmosphere is anyway negligible.","D":"Pressure of water vapour at a saturation level in the atmosphere prevents sweat from human bodies from evaporating."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Pressure of water vapour at a saturation level in the atmosphere prevents sweat from human bodies from evaporating.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"out_of_scope","C":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"We need to explain why higher humidity leads to people appearing to sweat more. Choice A: Higher temperature evaporating water vapour describes a different atmospheric condition — this is irrelevant to the stated phenomenon. Choice B: Sweating only during physical exertion is outside the scope of the argument and does not explain the humidity-sweat relationship. Choice C: Water vapour presence being negligible contradicts the premise that humidity levels are meaningfully high — this is incorrect. Choice D: CORRECT — at high humidity, the atmosphere is already saturated with water vapour. This prevents sweat from evaporating off the skin, causing it to accumulate visibly on the body, making people appear to sweat more. This is the correct physical explanation.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["paradox_resolution"]},{"id":"CR_E3_024","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"Last year, farmers in country X produced a cabbage crop that was one-and-a-half times as large as that produced in its neighbouring country Y. Yet the total quantity of cabbage available for sale to consumers in country X was smaller than were total quantities in country Y.","question":"Which of the following, if true, contributes most to an explanation of why there was less cabbage available for sale to consumers in country X than in country Y?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Country X had more land suitable for growing cabbage than did country Y.","B":"Cabbage was much more popular among consumers in country X than in country Y.","C":"Country Y exported more cabbage last year than country X.","D":"Farmers in country X consume a greater part of their cabbage crop than those in country Y."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Farmers in country X consume a greater part of their cabbage crop than those in country Y.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"out_of_scope","C":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"We need to explain why X produced more cabbage yet had less available for consumer sale. Something must be preventing a large portion of X's crop from reaching the market. Choice A: Land availability explains why X produces more, but does not explain why less reaches the market. Choice B: Higher consumer popularity of cabbage in X would increase demand but does not explain why supply available for sale is lower. Choice C: Country Y exporting more cabbage would reduce what is available in Y's market, making the paradox worse rather than explaining it. Choice D: CORRECT — if farmers in X consume a significantly greater portion of their own crop, less of X's larger harvest reaches the consumer market, directly explaining why market availability in X is lower than in Y despite higher production.","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"question_type":["paradox_resolution"]},{"id":"CR_E3_025","exercise":"exercise_3","passage":"Bird hits in the air when flights take off or land are a major cause of accidents involving aeroplanes. Hence it is advisable to relocate the sanctuary for emus, proposed to be constructed near the airport, to a distant place.","question":"Which of the following, if true, would show that the above advice to shift the proposed bird sanctuary is not logically founded?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Aeroplanes reduce their speed, while taking off or landing.","B":"Emus are large birds that can't fly high because of their body weight.","C":"There have been accidents due more to technical faults than to bird flights.","D":"Environmentalists believe that the proposed construction site is ideally suited for a bird sanctuary."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Emus are large birds that can't fly high because of their body weight.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The argument recommends relocating the emu sanctuary because bird hits cause aircraft accidents. We need to show this relocation advice is not logically founded. Choice A: Aircraft speed during takeoff and landing does not affect whether emus pose a collision risk. Choice B: CORRECT — if emus cannot fly high due to their body weight, they cannot reach the altitude at which aircraft operate during takeoff and landing. There is therefore no risk of bird hits from emus, making the advice to relocate the sanctuary logically unfounded. Choice C: Other accidents being more common due to technical faults does not rule out the possibility that emus near the airport could still cause bird-hit accidents. Choice D: The environmentalists' view on site suitability is outside the scope of the safety argument.","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"question_type":["weaken"]}]`);export{e as default};
